## Building a Smarter Vending Machine

A vending machine has items in stock. When someone buys something, the result can be success with change, out of stock, or insufficient funds.

### The Item Struct

```xiom
type Item = {
  name: Str;
  price: Float64;
  stock: Int;
}
```

### The VendResult Enum

```xiom
enum VendResult {
  Success(item: Str, change: Float64),
  OutOfStock,
  InsufficientFunds,
}
```

`Success` carries the item name and any change. The error variants carry no data — the meaning is in the variant name.

### The Vend Function

```xiom
fn vend(item: Item, money: Float64) -> VendResult {
  if item.stock <= 0 {
    return VendResult.OutOfStock;
  }
  if money < item.price {
    return VendResult.InsufficientFunds;
  }
  let change = money - item.price;
  return VendResult.Success(item.name, change);
}
```

### Using the Machine

```xiom
let chips = Item{ name: "Chips"; price: 1.50; stock: 3; };

let r1 = vend(chips, 2.00);  // Success("Chips", 0.50)
let r2 = vend(chips, 1.00);  // InsufficientFunds

match r1 {
  Success(name, change) => {
    // Dispense item, return change
  }
  OutOfStock => {
    // Show "Sold out" on screen
  }
  InsufficientFunds => {
    // Show "Insert more money"
  }
}
```

### Why Combine Structs and Enums?

Structs model the static data (what's in the machine). Enums model the dynamic outcomes (what happened during the transaction). Together they cover the full system.

